sh - run commands and shell scripts

SYNOPSIS
    sh [-c command [name [argument ...]]]
    sh script [argument ...]

DESCRIPTION
    sh reads commands from a file, standard input, or the terminal.
    Separate commands with a newline or semicolon. Use | for a pipeline,
    && for success, || for failure, and & for a background job.
    Parentheses run a list in a child shell.

    Single quotes preserve literal text. Double quotes preserve spaces
    and allow variable expansion. Use NAME=value, export NAME, and unset
    NAME to manage variables. The executable search uses PATH.

    Scripts support if/elif/else/fi, while or until/do/done, and
    for name in words/do/done. Use break or continue inside a loop.
    $0 through $9 refer to arguments, $# is the argument count, $? is the
    last result, and $$ is the shell PID. Use "$@" to pass arguments on.

    Use <, >, >> and 2> for files, and 2>&1 to copy a descriptor.
    Built-ins include cd, umask, set, export, unset, exec, exit, jobs, fg,
    bg, break, continue, and :. Use exec program [argument ...] to replace
    the current shell process. Use exec with only redirections to change
    the shell's file descriptors.

EXAMPLES
    for item in alpha beta; do echo "$item"; done

SEE ALSO
    env(1), test(1), execve(2), shell(5)
